"Connects each record across parallel numeric axes to reveal multivariate profiles and relationships between adjacent variables.\n\nUse when: Records have several numeric variables and profile comparison matters. Readers can inspect a selected subset or a manageable number of records.\n\nAvoid when: Dense overplotting or incompatible scale interpretations make individual profiles impossible to follow.\n\nVisual grammar: Each vertical axis encodes one variable with its own declared scale. One polyline encodes one record across axes in a fixed order.\n\nBuild: Choose domain-relevant variables, axis directions, and a documented normalization policy. Place axes with enough spacing for labels above or below the plot. Connect matched record values and highlight only selected profiles or groups.\n\nCheck: Check every segment joins values from the same record. Verify all axis ranges and reversed directions are explicit. Confirm normalization preserves the comparisons the audience is meant to make.\n\nPitfalls: Crossings encode opposite adjacent-variable orderings and are intrinsic to the method. Prefer a heatmap or scatterplot matrix by default when crossings dominate; never reroute polylines around other measured values.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic vehicle profiles across price, efficiency, capacity, and range, highlighting three models against muted background records.\n\nAlternatives: Heatmap, Scatterplot matrix, Radar chart."
